From def7eacf0bae75190228e84ccf0153cd2092c052 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Malakhouski <malakhouski.ilya@planetvpn.dev>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 16:09:45 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Sync with upstream openvpn-build, rebase the XOR patch, add
 licensing

Squashed equivalent of the local sync work (merge of OpenVPN/openvpn-build
master plus four fork commits), flattened into one patch so that it can be
applied on top of the current origin/master (be3ae1b).

Upstream restructured substantially since this fork was last synced:

- OpenVPN, openvpn-gui, vcpkg and ovpn-backports are now git submodules under
  src/, pinned to exact commits, instead of being checked out by CI.
- generic/ and windows-nsis/ (mingw cross-compilation and NSIS installers) were
  removed upstream as unmaintained; this fork follows, so the legacy ccpp.yml
  workflow and the dead .travis.yml / .appveyor.yml go with them.
- The MSI build now targets OpenVPN 2.8_git with WiX 3.14.1 on windows-2022,
  and signing moved to Jsign/Google Cloud KMS.

The Tunnelblick XOR ("scramble") patch set was refreshed from Tunnelblick's
openvpn-2.7.6 patches and rebased onto the pinned src/openvpn commit, because
upstream OpenVPN moved further: link_socket_read() now returns ssize_t, the tree
was reformatted, and the SHOW_* hunk moved from options.c to options_show.c
(openvpn 6d18f2ce). strlen() results are cast and byte arithmetic is masked
explicitly so the patch compiles warning-free under -Wconversion; behaviour is
unchanged.

New 07-openvpn-xor-disable-dco.diff makes --scramble disable data channel
offload the way --fragment and --http-proxy do. DCO is default on Windows since
2.6 and handles the data channel inside the driver, so without this only the
control channel would be obfuscated and the connection would fail against an
xor-patched server.

CI applies the patches to src/openvpn before building, drops the jobs that only
work on OpenVPN's own infrastructure (AWS t_client tests, MSI upload, Debian
packages), gates the dependency-graph workflow on the repository owner, and
gains a patch-check job that applies the patches, builds patched OpenVPN on
Linux, runs its unit tests and asserts that every scramble method parses and
that DCO gets disabled.

Verified on the pinned submodule commit: warning-free build, 18/18 unit tests,
all four scramble methods accepted, and an obfuscated tunnel between two patched
instances completes its handshake and stays up - while a peer pair with
mismatched scramble settings does not connect.

Adds LICENSE (GPL v2) and states in README.md that this is an unofficial
repository, unaffiliated with OpenVPN, Inc. or Tunnelblick, that scramble is
obfuscation and not security, and that DCO is off as a consequence. The MSI
packages carry their own upgrade codes and are published as
"openvpn-windows-xor (unofficial build)" so they cannot silently upgrade, or be
upgraded by, official OpenVPN installations.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
